  "_notes": {
    "предимство": "Right of way is 'geçiş hakkı' (KTK Art. 57, 74: 'ilk geçiş hakkı'). 'Geçiş üstünlüğü' is reserved for the special regime of emergency vehicles (KTK Art. 61), which is exactly the Bulgarian 'специален режим на движение'. Keeping them apart preserves the Bulgarian distinction.",
    "изпреварване / заобикаляне / разминаване": "sollama (same direction) / duran araç veya engelin yanından geçme (stationary vehicle or obstacle; the bracket is not written, only 'yanından geçme' with its object) / karşılaşma (oncoming). 'Geçme' alone is avoided for overtaking because it is ambiguous in Turkish.",
    "спиране / престой / паркиране": "durma (any stop) / duraklama (KTK: stop to let people on or off or to load/unload) / park etme. When 'спиране' means braking (ABS, braking distance) it is 'frenleme'.",
    "ППС / МПС / превозно средство / автомобил": "Turkish law has the same nesting as Bulgarian: araç (any vehicle) ⊃ motorlu araç (any motorised vehicle, incl. tractors and work machines) ⊃ motorlu taşıt (motor vehicle for carrying people or goods). So ППС = araç, МПС = motorlu araç (parallel to Bulgarian ППС/МПС), превозно средство (generic) = taşıt, автомобил = otomobil in everyday use (cars in pictures, the car in technical topics) but 'motorlu taşıt' where the Bulgarian means the legal class that includes lorries and buses (iaaa-5324…5329 'Кое … е автомобил?', 'автомобил от категория С', a vehicle over 3500 kg). лек автомобил = binek otomobil; товарен автомобил = yük taşıtı (not 'kamyon', which in Turkish law means > 3,5 t only).",
    "правоспособност": "Possession of an entitlement is expressed with 'X sınıfı sürücü belgesi' (natural Turkish legal usage; the pivot also equates 'без правоспособност' with 'without a driving licence'). Loss of entitlement is 'sürme hakkının kaybı' so that it cannot be read as losing the document.",
    "контролни точки": "'kontrol puanları' (deducted points), never 'ceza puanı' (the Turkish system adds penalty points, the opposite logic).",
    "категория / клас": "Licence categories are 'X sınıfı' with the Latin code (B sınıfı, C1E sınıfı); national categories Tkt, Ttb, Ttm. Bus classes are 'A sınıfı otobüs', 'I. sınıf'.",
    "units and numbers": "Decimal comma (3,5 t); SI symbols kept; suffixes are avoided on unit symbols where possible by using 'en fazla', 'en az', '… üzerinde', '… altında'. Dates keep their digits: 'След 12.12.24г.' = '12.12.24 tarihinden sonra'.",
    "ЗДвП": "Translated as 'Karayolu Trafik Kanunu' (no abbreviation, to avoid confusion with Turkey's 2918 sayılı Karayolları Trafik Kanunu, 'KTK'). Citation: 'Karayolu Trafik Kanununun 157. maddesinin 4. fıkrası'.",
    "address": "The candidate is addressed with 'siz' (Ne yaparsınız? Nasıl davranırsınız?). First-person answers stay first person (… geçerim).",
    "colon stems": "A stem ending in ':' keeps the colon; Turkish word order requires the stem to be built as a topic or a cataphoric clause so that each answer completes it (e.g. 'Sürücü:' + 'durmak zorundadır'; 'Bu, şu anlama gelir:' + '…').",
    "yes/no answers": "да/не = evet/hayır; правилно/неправилно and вярно/невярно = doğru/yanlış; разрешено/забранено = izin verilir/yasaktır in running text; as a standalone answer after a stem such as 'X-mak:' (Bulgarian '… е:') = serbesttir/yasaktır, the same everywhere; забранено във всички случаи = her durumda yasaktır.",
    "quotation marks": "Turkish “ ” for all quoted names and sign texts.",
    "warning sign vs hazard signal": "The road-sign class 'predupreditelen znak' is 'uyarı işareti'; 'tehlike uyarı işareti / ışıkları' is reserved for the hazard warning signal of the vehicle lights ('avarien signal'), so the two never share a name.",
    "road classes": "автомагистрала = otoyol (140 km/h for B); скоростен път = ekspres yol (expressway, 120 km/h for B); автомобилен път = motorlu taşıt yolu (the signed road for motor vehicles, Vienna sign E,6a, white car on blue, with its own rules: no mopeds, tractors or bicycles, no U-turn or reversing, 90 km/h outside built-up areas). Three distinct terms; 'karayolu' (any road; used for 'road transport') is never used for a road class.",
    "carriageway": "платно за движение = taşıt yolu (the part used by vehicles); пътно платно = yol platformu (the wider formation: carriageway + shoulders + islands, iaaa-5230). Never interchanged; iaaa-5412/5413 offer both as separate answers.",
    "sign names": "Quoted sign and signal names keep the Bulgarian quotation and capitalisation and are rendered with the same Turkish wording everywhere (e.g. “Geçiş yasaktır”, “Geçiş serbesttir”, “Dikkat”, “Dikkat, dur!”, “Yol boşsa geç”)."
  }
